// Mutual recursion by backpatching: one cell per member, each at its own type,
// each written once. The only encoding here that is O(1) in the size of the
// group and asks for no `Rec`, no product and no tag -- and the one to reach
// for last.
//
// It needs a seed before it has an answer, which is a synthesis problem the
// types do not solve. `diverge` is what makes the seeds writable at all: bottom
// at an arrow type, and a *value* rather than a running loop, so `ref!` may
// hold one. `ref/cell.ga` has the general form as `fixKnot`, and says what the
// bottom seed does and does not buy.
//
// Requires resolve against the search path, and `data/` sits above this
// directory rather than below it, so the entry needs one:
//     deno task run stdlib/rec/mutual-ref.ga -I stdlib
#require "rec/fix.ga"
#require "data/nat.ga"
#require "data/pair.ga"
#require "data/list.ga"
#require "ref/cell.ga"

// The instantiations are forced. Nothing in `ref!`'s argument list constrains
// the seed, so left alone its variables solve at `unknown -> never` and the
// backpatch below is refused -- the cell would learn what it holds one line too
// late. Same shape as `toList` in `uses-both.ga`.
let ralt = ref!(diverge[Nat, List[Nat]]())
let rtag = ref!(diverge[Nat, Nat]())

// Reading through the cell is what defers the recursive call, so these two are
// what the bodies below refer to each other by.
let alt = fn (n: Nat) -> get!(ralt)(n)
let tag = fn (n: Nat) -> get!(rtag)(n)

// The writes need no annotation: the cell is checked first and says what the
// function it is handed takes.
let _ = set!(ralt, fn (n) -> match n with
  | Z -> Nil()
  | S(k) -> Cons(tag(k), alt(k)))

let _ = set!(rtag, fn (n) -> match n with
  | Z -> Z
  | S(k) -> length(alt(k)))

// Nothing in the types says the two writes above happened. Call `alt` between
// the allocation and its `set!` and it diverges rather than answering, which is
// the whole of what the bottom seed guarantees.
Pair(alt(S(S(Z))), tag(S(S(S(Z)))))
